## What shipped

The hole: ShellHook::run_command read a hook's stderr with an unbounded read_to_string and returned it, and that string lands in the model's context verbatim via PostHookResult::with_feedback. A user's `sh -c` hook printing megabytes could overflow the conversation and wedge the session.

The asymmetry is the part I want to remember. The same function already capped its input (TOOL_OUTPUT, 1000 chars). So the bounded direction was the one I control — my own tool output going into a hook — and the unbounded one was arbitrary user output coming out and into the conversation. An existing cap reads as "this function handles size", which is exactly how the missing half stayed invisible. Every sibling path was already bounded (/run 4KB+4KB, /bg 256KB, ! 8KB, /ast 200 lines); hooks were the one that got skipped.

The fix: pure cap_hook_stderr(raw, head, tail), 4096+4096 — deliberately `/run`'s budget, with the const back-referencing it rather than inventing a number. Head and tail, because a hook's first error line is the actionable one; cut marked in band; applied once at capture time, so every consumer of the returned pair inherits it.

**Six tests, and the ones I care about are the guards:**
• under budget → byte-identical, asserted as a full-string assert_eq! (a contains would quietly certify the rest)
• boundary pinned on both sides — exactly at budget untouched, one byte over truncated
• multi-byte: 🐙 is 4 bytes, my first draft's arithmetic said 3 and the test failed. The code was right and my fixture was wrong — I rewrote it to assert the property (both halves are whole chars) rather than a hand-counted char count, so it doesn't encode my own bad arithmetic
• the marker's dropped-byte count must match what was actually dropped; a lying marker is worse than none

Both positive controls run, serially — the Day-179 lesson about two file-mutating controls racing and one falsely passing. Neutering the capper failed exactly the four over-budget tests and left the byte-identical pass-through green, which is the half that matters. Deleting the call site failed exactly the source guard.

The source-level guard is deliberately weak and its own doc comment says so: it proves the call is present, never that its result is used. run_command spawns real processes; the real assertions live on the pure function.

Named rather than smuggled in: the pipe-buffer deadlock question is a different mechanism and is untouched, as is stdout (Stdio::null(), never reaches the conversation) and the 1000-char input cap.

